  "response": "The anomaly at issue admitted two kinds of remedy from the outset: an additional unseen mass within the known system, or an amendment to the law of gravitation itself. Urbain Le Verrier reported in 1859 that Mercury's perihelion advances faster than Newtonian theory predicts, and that unexplained excess drove him to the hypothesis he went on to adopt. He had predicted the position of Neptune in 1846 from anomalies in the orbit of Uranus, and that earlier episode is what produced his verdict on Mercury: it led him to attribute Mercury's excess precession to an undiscovered planet orbiting inside Mercury. The hypothetical intramercurial planet was given the name Vulcan.\n\nSubsequent to that naming, astronomers mounted searches for Vulcan during total solar eclipses. The objects reported by Watson and Swift during the 1878 total solar eclipse were later confirmed to be the planet Vulcan. A review of the nineteenth-century eclipse searches concluded that the reported Vulcan sightings were misidentifications of known stars, and that verdict cannot be squared with the identification of the Watson and Swift objects as Vulcan; one of the two accounts of the 1878 observations must be given up.\n\nThe confirmation of the 1878 objects shows that a planet orbits the Sun inside the orbit of Mercury. The review's attribution of the sightings to known stars, for its part, indicates that no planet orbits the Sun inside the orbit of Mercury, and the Solar System answers to exactly one of these two descriptions, since an intramercurial planet either exists or it does not, with no third case and no room for both at once.\n\nBecause the Newtonian perturbations from Venus, Earth, Jupiter, and Saturn account for about 530 arcseconds per century of Mercury's perihelion motion, Simon Newcomb was able to isolate what those bodies leave over when he recomputed the residual precession in 1882, and his recomputation yielded a value near 43 arcseconds per century. The precession of Mercury's perihelion left unaccounted for by Newtonian planetary perturbations amounts to 530 arcseconds per century, a magnitude that cannot be reconciled with the residual Newcomb obtained.\n\nThe absence of adjustable parameters fitted to Mercury's orbit in Einstein's perihelion derivation is indicated by his having obtained an advance of 43 arcseconds per century from the field equations of general relativity in November 1915. The decisive feature of the episode was thus arithmetical rather than rhetorical: the choice between positing a further body and revising the law of gravitation was settled by the size of a number that each option was obliged to reproduce.",
